# Converting JSON String to Object in JavaScript

## Using `JSON.parse()`

The standard way to convert a JSON string into a JavaScript object is using `JSON.parse()`.

```javascript
const jsonString = '{"name": "Alice", "age": 30, "city": "New York"}';
const obj = JSON.parse(jsonString);

console.log(obj.name);  // "Alice"
console.log(obj.age);   // 30
console.log(obj.city);  // "New York"
```

## Basic Example with Nested Data

```javascript
const jsonString = `{
  "name": "Bob",
  "age": 25,
  "hobbies": ["reading", "coding"],
  "address": {
    "street": "123 Main St",
    "zip": "10001"
  }
}`;

const obj = JSON.parse(jsonString);

console.log(obj.hobbies[0]);       // "reading"
console.log(obj.address.zip);      // "10001"
console.log(typeof obj);           // "object"
```

## Error Handling

Always handle potential parsing errors:

```javascript
const jsonString = '{"name": "Alice", "age": 30'; // Invalid JSON (missing closing brace)

try {
  const obj = JSON.parse(jsonString);
  console.log(obj);
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Invalid JSON:", error.message);
  // Output: Invalid JSON: Unexpected end of JSON input
}
```

## Key Points

| Aspect | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Method** | `JSON.parse()` |
| **Input** | A valid JSON-formatted **string** |
| **Output** | A JavaScript **object** (or array, number, etc.) |
| **Inverse** | `JSON.stringify()` converts an object back to a JSON string |
| **Security** | `JSON.parse()` is **safe** — it only parses data, not code (unlike `eval()`) |

## Common Mistakes

```javascript
// ✅ Correct
const obj = JSON.parse('{"key": "value"}');

// ❌ Wrong - don't use eval() (security risk!)
const obj = eval('({"key": "value"});');

// ❌ Wrong - passing an object instead of a

