# Replace All Occurrences of a Substring

Here are examples in several popular programming languages:

---

## Python
```python
text = "hello world, hello python, hello everyone"
new_text = text.replace("hello", "hi")
print(new_text)
# Output: "hi world, hi python, hi everyone"
```

## JavaScript
```javascript
const text = "hello world, hello python, hello everyone";
const new_text = text.replace(/hello/g, "hi");
console.log(new_text);
// Output: "hi world, hi python, hi everyone"
```

## Java
```java
String text = "hello world, hello python, hello everyone";
String newText = text.replace("hello", "hi");
System.out.println(newText);
// Output: "hi world, hi python, hi everyone"
```

## C#
```csharp
string text = "hello world, hello python, hello everyone";
string newText = text.Replace("hello", "hi");
Console.WriteLine(newText);
// Output: "hi world, hi python, hi everyone"
```

## C++
```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

std::string replaceAll(std::string str, const std::string& from, const std::string& to) {
    size_t startPos = 0;
    while ((startPos = str.find(from, startPos)) != std::string::npos) {
        str.replace(startPos, from.length(), to);
        startPos += to.length();
    }
    return str;
}

int main() {
    std::string text = "hello world, hello python, hello everyone";
    std::string newText = replaceAll(text, "hello", "hi");
    std::cout << newText << std::endl;
    // Output: "hi world, hi python, hi everyone"
    return 0;
}
```

## Key Notes
- **Python**, **Java**, and **C#** have built-in methods that replace **all** occurrences by default.
- **JavaScript**'s `.replace()` only replaces the **first** occurrence unless you use a **global regex** (`/pattern/g`).
- **C++** does not have a built-in `replaceAll`, so a custom function is needed.

