# Sorting an Array in JavaScript

JavaScript provides several ways to sort arrays. Here's a comprehensive guide:

## 1. Basic Sorting (Lexicographic by Default)

The `sort()` method sorts elements **as strings** by default, which can lead to unexpected results with numbers.

```javascript
// String sorting (works correctly)
['banana', 'apple', 'cherry'].sort();
// => ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']

// NUMBER SORTING PROBLEM
[10, 5, 40, 25, 100, 1].sort();
// => [1, 10, 100, 25, 40, 5]  ❌ Incorrect!
```

## 2. Sorting Numbers Correctly

Use a **compare function**:

```javascript
// Ascending order
[10, 5, 40, 25, 100, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b);
// => [1, 5, 10, 25, 40, 100]  ✅

// Descending order
[10, 5, 40, 25, 100, 1].sort((a, b) => b - a);
// => [100, 40, 25, 10, 5, 1]  ✅
```

## 3. Sorting Strings (Case-Insensitive)

```javascript
// Case-insensitive ascending
['Banana', 'apple', 'Cherry'].sort((a, b) =>
  a.toLowerCase().localeCompare(b.toLowerCase())
);
// => ['apple', 'Banana', 'Cherry']  ✅
```

## 4. Sorting Objects by Property

```javascript
const users = [
  { name: 'Alice', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 25 },
  { name: 'Charlie', age: 35 }
];

// Sort by age (ascending)
users.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);
// => [{ name: 'Bob', age: 25 }, { name: 'Alice', age: 30 }, { name: 'Charlie', age: 35 }]

