console.log(max); // 9
```

> ⚠️ **Limitation**: May fail with very large arrays due to call stack limits.

---

### 2. Using `reduce()`
```javascript
const numbers = [3, 1, 7, 2, 9];
const max = numbers.reduce((a, b) => Math.max(a, b));
console.log(max); // 9
```

> ✅ Safe for large arrays; works well for any size.

---

### 3. Using a Simple Loop
```javascript
const numbers = [3, 1, 7, 2, 9];
let max = numbers[0];
for (let i = 1; i < numbers.length; i++) {
  if (numbers[i] > max) {
    max = numbers[i];
  }
}
console.log(max); // 9
```

> ✅ Most performant for large arrays.

---

### 4. Using `Math.max.apply()`
```javascript
const numbers = [3, 1, 7, 2, 9];
const max = Math.max.apply(null, numbers);
console.log(max); // 9
```

> ⚠️ Also has stack size limitations like the spread operator.

---

### Handling Edge Cases

```javascript
// Empty array
const numbers = [];
const max = numbers.length > 0 ? Math.max(...numbers) : undefined;

// Array with negative numbers
const numbers = [-5, -2, -9, -1];
const max = Math.max(...numbers); // -1

// Non-numeric values (filters them out or returns NaN)
const numbers = [3, 'a', 7, null, 2, 9];
const max = Math.max(...numbers.filter(n => typeof n === 'number')); // 9
```

