# Trim Whitespace from String Ends in JavaScript

## Using Built-in `trim()`

The simplest and most common approach:

```javascript
const str = "  hello world  ";
const trimmed = str.trim(); // "hello world"
```

### Available Methods

| Method | Description | Example |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| `.trim()` | Removes whitespace from **both** ends | `"  hi  ".trim()` → `"hi"` |
| `.trimStart()` / `.trimLeft()` | Removes whitespace from the **start** only | `"  hi  ".trimStart()` → `"hi  "` |
| `.trimEnd()` / `.trimRight()` | Removes whitespace from the **end** only | `"  hi  ".trimEnd()` → `"  hi"` |

## What Counts as Whitespace?

`trim()` removes all characters with Unicode whitespace values, including:
- Spaces (` `)
- Tabs (`\t`)
- Newlines (`\n`, `\r`)
- Non-breaking spaces (`\u00A0`)
- And other Unicode whitespace characters

## Custom Trim (Specific Characters)

If you need to trim only specific characters (e.g., spaces only, not tabs):

```javascript
// Trim only spaces (not tabs/newlines)
const str = "  hello \t world  ";

// Using regex
const trimmed = str.replace(/^\s+|\s+$/g, " "); 
// For only spaces:
const trimmedSpaces = str.replace(/^ +| +$/g, "");
```

## Browser Support

All these methods (`trim`, `trimStart`, `trimEnd`) are supported in all modern browsers and Node.js. For older environments (e.g., IE11), you can use a polyfill:

```javascript
// Polyfill for IE11 (which only supports .trim())
if (!String.prototype.trimStart) {
  String.prototype.trimStart = function() {
    return this.replace(/^\s+/, '');
  };
}
if (!String.prototype.trimEnd) {
  String.prototype.trimEnd = function() {
    return this.replace(/\s+$/, '');
  };
}
```

