# Comparing Two Objects for Deep Equality

Deep equality means comparing objects recursively — all nested properties, arrays, and values must match exactly, regardless of reference identity.

## Why Not `===` or `JSON.stringify()`?

| Approach | Problem |
|---|---|
| `===` | Compares **references**, not content |
| `JSON.stringify()` | Loses `undefined`, `Date`, `RegExp`, circular refs, key order sensitivity |

---

## Implementation (Pure JavaScript)

```javascript
function deepEqual(a, b) {
  // 1. Strict equality covers primitives and same reference
  if (a === b) return true;

  // 2. Handle null/undefined (both non-null after === check)
  if (a == null || b == null) return false;

  // 3. Type mismatch
  if (typeof a !== typeof b) return false;

  // 4. Both are objects (including arrays, dates, regex, etc.)
  if (typeof a === 'object') {
    // Handle Date
    if (a instanceof Date && b instanceof Date) {
      return a.getTime() === b.getTime();
    }
    // Handle RegExp
    if (a instanceof RegExp && b instanceof RegExp) {
      return a.toString() === b.toString();
    }
    // Handle Plain Objects & Arrays
    if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) return false;

    const keysA = Object.keys(a);
    const keysB = Object.keys(b);
    if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;

    // Check every key
    for (const key of keysA) {
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(b, key)) return false;
      if (!deepEqual(a[key], b[key])) return false;
    }

    return true;
  }

  return false;
}
```

## Usage Examples

```javascript
