# Dropping All Tables in MySQL

## Important Warning

⚠️ **This is a destructive operation** that permanently deletes all data in the specified database. Ensure you have backups before proceeding.

## Approach 1: Drop All Tables in the Current Database

```sql
-- First, disable foreign key checks to avoid ordering issues
SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 0;

-- Generate and execute DROP TABLE statements
SELECT CONCAT('DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `', table_name, '`;')
FROM information_schema.tables
WHERE table_schema = DATABASE();

-- Re-enable foreign key checks
SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 1;
```

> **Note:** The above generates the commands. To execute them, you can:

### Execute via a Script (MySQL 8.0+)

```sql
SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 0;

-- Loop through and drop all tables
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS table1, table2, ...;  -- manually generated

SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 1;
```

### Using a Stored Procedure (Works on most MySQL versions)

```sql
DELIMITER //

CREATE PROCEDURE DropAllTables()
BEGIN
    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT FALSE;
    DECLARE tableName VARCHAR(255);
    DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR
        SELECT table_name
        FROM information_schema.tables
        WHERE table_schema = DATABASE();

    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = TRUE;

    SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 0;

    OPEN cur;

    read_loop: LOOP
        FETCH cur INTO tableName;
        IF done THEN
            LEAVE read_loop;
        END IF;
        SET @sql = CONCAT('DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `', tableName, '`');
        PREPARE stmt FROM @sql;
        EXECUTE stmt;
        DEALLOCATE PREPARE stmt;
    END LOOP;

    CLOSE cur;
    SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS = 1;
END //

DELIMITER ;

-- Call the procedure
CALL DropAllTables();

-- Clean up
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS DropAllTables;
```

## Approach 2: Drop and Recreate the Entire Database (Simplest)

```sql
-- Drop the entire database
DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS your_database_name;

-- Recreate it
CREATE DATABASE your_database_name;

-- Use it
USE your_database_name;
```

