# Creating an Empty List in Java

There are several ways to create an empty list in Java:

## 1. Using `ArrayList` (Most Common)

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
// or with initial capacity
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(0);
```

## 2. Using `Collections.emptyList()` (Immutable)

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

List<String> list = Collections.emptyList();
// Note: This returns an immutable list. Adding elements will throw UnsupportedOperationException.
```

## 3. Using `List.of()` (Java 9+, Immutable)

```java
import java.util.List;

List<String> list = List.of();
// Immutable. Adding elements will throw UnsupportedOperationException.
```

## 4. Using `Arrays.asList()` with a zero-length array

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

List<String> list = Arrays.asList();
// This returns a fixed-size list. You cannot add or remove elements.
```

## Comparison

| Method | Mutable? | Thread-Safe? | Java Version |
|---|---|---|---|
| `new ArrayList<>()` | ✅ Yes | ❌ No | All |
| `Collections.emptyList()` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | All |
| `List.of()` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | 9+ |
| `Arrays.asList()` | ❌ Fixed-size | ❌ No | All |

## Recommendation

- **For a mutable list**: Use `new ArrayList<>()`
- **For an immutable list**: Use `List.of()` (Java 9+) or `Collections.emptyList()`

