# Checking if a List is Empty in Java

There are several ways to check if a `List` is empty in Java. Here are the most common approaches:

## 1. Using `isEmpty()` (Recommended)

```java
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

if (list.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println("List is empty");
} else {
    System.out.println("List has " + list.size() + " elements");
}
```

## 2. Using `size() == 0`

```java
if (list.size() == 0) {
    System.out.println("List is empty");
}
```

## Key Differences

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| `isEmpty()` | More readable, semantically clearer | Slightly newer API (Java 2) |
| `size() == 0` | Works in very old Java versions | Less expressive intent |

## ⚠️ Important: Handle `null`

Both methods will throw a **`NullPointerException`** if the list itself is `null`. Always check for `null` first:

```java
if (list == null || list.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println("List is either null or empty");
}
```

## Tip: Java 8+ Optional Utility

For a concise null-safe check, you can use:

```java
import java.util.Optional;

Optional.ofNullable(list).map(List::isEmpty).orElse(false);
// Returns true if list is empty or null, false otherwise
```

## Best Practice

> **Use `isEmpty()`** — it is the most idiomatic and readable way to check if a list has no elements. Always guard against `null` if the list might not be initialized.

