|-------------------|------------------|-----------------|---------------------------|
| `Object.keys()`   | Array of keys    | ✅ Yes          | When you only need keys   |
| `Object.entries()`| Array of `[k,v]` | ✅ Yes          | When you need keys & values |
| `for...in`        | Keys (iterative) | �

# Checking if a String is Empty in JavaScript

## Basic Check

```javascript
let str = "";

if (str === "") {
  console.log("String is empty");
}
```

## Common Approaches

### 1. Check for Empty String (`""`)
```javascript
if (str === "") { ... }
// or
if (str.length === 0) { ... }
```

### 2. Check for Empty or Whitespace-Only String
```javascript
if (str.trim() === "") { ... }
// or
if (/^\s*$/.test(str)) { ... }
```

### 3. Check for Falsy Values (empty, `null`, `undefined`, `0`, `false`)
```javascript
if (!str) { ... }
```
> ⚠️ **Caution**: This also treats `null`, `undefined`, `0`, and `false` as "empty."

## Best Practice: Helper Function

```javascript
function isEmpty(str) {
  return str === "" || str.trim() === "";
}

// Usage
console.log(isEmpty(""));        // true
console.log(isEmpty("   "));     // true
console.log(isEmpty("hello"));   // false
```

## Summary

| Check | Catches | Misses |
|---|---|---|
| `str === ""` | Only `""` | `"   "`, `null`, `undefined` |
| `str.trim() === ""` | `""`, `"   "`, etc. | `null`, `undefined` |
| `!str` | `""`, `null`, `undefined`, `0`, `false` | `"   "` (whitespace) |
| `str?.trim() === ""` | `""`, whitespace, handles `null`/`undefined` safely | — |

### ✅ Recommended Safe Check (handles all edge cases):
```javascript
function isEmpty(str) {
  return str === null || str === undefined || String(str).trim() === "";
}
```

This approach:
- Handles `null` and `undefined` safely
- Trims whitespace
- Converts to string if needed
- Returns `true` only when the string is truly empty

